DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The eeffiibboooottmmggrr program manipulates how UEFI Boot Managers boot the sys‐
     tem.  It can create and destroy methods for booting along with activating
     or deactivating them.  It can also change the defined order of boot meth‐
     ods.  It can create a temporary boot (BootNext) variable that references
     a boot method to be tried once upon the next boot.

     The UEFI standard defines how hosts may control what is used to bootstrap
     the system.  Each method is encapsulated within a persistent UEFI vari‐
     able, stored by the UEFI BIOS of the form BBoooott_X_X_X_X (where XXXX are upper‐
     case hexadecimal digits).  These variables are numbered, each describing
     where to load the bootstrap program from, and whether or not the method
     is active (used for booting, otherwise the method will be skipped).  The
     order of these methods is controlled by another variable, BBoooottOOrrddeerr.  The
     currently booted method is communicated using BBoooottCCuurrrreenntt.  A global
     timeout can also be set.

     eeffiibboooottmmggrr requires that the kernel module efirt(9) module be present or
     loaded to get and set these non-volatile variables.

     The following options are available:

     --aa ----aaccttiivvaattee
             Activate the given _b_o_o_t_n_u_m boot entry, or the new entry when used
             with --cc.

     --AA ----ddeeaaccttiivvaattee
             Deactivate the given _b_o_o_t_n_u_m boot entry.

     --bb ----bboooottnnuumm _b_o_o_t_n_u_m
             When creating or modifying an entry, use _b_o_o_t_n_u_m as the index.
             When creating a new entry, fail if it already exists.

     --BB ----ddeelleettee
             Delete the given _b_o_o_t_n_u_m boot entry.

     --cc ----ccrreeaattee
             Create a new BBoooott variable (aka method or entry).

     --DD ----ddrryy--rruunn
             Process but do not change any variables.

     --EE ----eesspp
             Print the FreeBSD path to the ESP device, derived from the EFI
             variables _B_o_o_t_C_u_r_r_e_n_t and _B_o_o_t_X_X_X_X.  This is the ESP partition
             used by UEFI to boot the current instance of the system.  If --dd
             ----ddeevviiccee--ppaatthh is specified, the UEFI device path to the ESP is
             reported instead.  If --pp ----uunniixx--ppaatthh is specified, the mount
             point of the ESP is reported instead.

     --ff ----ffww--uuii, --FF ----nnoo--ffww--uuii
             Set or clear the request to the system firmware to stop in its
             user interface on the next boot.

     --kk ----kkeerrnneell _k_e_r_n_e_l
             The path to and name of the kernel.

     --ll ----llooaaddeerr _l_o_a_d_e_r
             The path to and name of the loader.

     --LL ----llaabbeell _l_a_b_e_l
             An optional description for the method.

     --nn ----bboooottnneexxtt
             Set _b_o_o_t_n_u_m boot entry as the BBoooottNNeexxtt variable.

     --NN ----ddeelleettee--bboooottnneexxtt
             Delete the BBoooottNNeexxtt optional variable.

     --oo ----bboooottoorrddeerr _b_o_o_t_o_r_d_e_r
             Set BBoooottOOrrddeerr variable to the given comma delimited set of
